WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.856 --> 00:00:02.702
Where should the support team focus?

2
00:00:02.989 --> 00:00:05.758
The analyst brings the incident
history into one view.

3
00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:09.507
Reporting has the largest share,
but Invoicing is close behind.

4
00:00:10.157 --> 00:00:13.133
Priority and status add
context to the raw counts.

5
00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.938
The follow-up tests whether
this is a repeat problem.

6
00:00:25.164 --> 00:00:27.073
The cases span almost two years,

7
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.487
and two subjects describe
similar dashboard slowness.

8
00:00:31.052 --> 00:00:32.949
That is a useful lead to investigate,

9
00:00:33.057 --> 00:00:34.839
rather than a confirmed diagnosis.

10
00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:44.643
Now open a source.

11
00:00:45.092 --> 00:00:48.281
The original ticket, its
recorded date, priority,

12
00:00:48.463 --> 00:00:51.012
and status remain visible
alongside the answer.

13
00:00:51.791 --> 00:00:55.264
The calculation is just as
accessible: fifty-eight reporting

14
00:00:55.289 --> 00:00:58.650
incidents divided by two hundred
and sixty-nine total incidents.

15
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.044
The result is a focused handoff.

16
00:01:07.482 --> 00:01:11.482
Review symptoms and closure quality,
connect confirmed product issues,

17
00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:14.212
and measure recurrence after an approved fix.

18
00:01:14.804 --> 00:01:17.932
The conversation has moved from
a broad question to an owner,

19
00:01:18.132 --> 00:01:20.980
an investigation, and a
way to assess the outcome.
